// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//  READINGS — the one part of the atlas that is the reader's own opinion.
//
//  READINGS_AUTHOR is the name shown above every reading:
//  "Bakhshi" appears as "Bakhshi’s Reading". Anyone who copies the
//  atlas can put their own name here.
//
//  Edit the text between the backticks ` ` for any entry.
//  A blank line starts a new paragraph.
//  Keep the entry names on the left exactly as they are.
//  Leave a reading empty (``) to show "No reading yet".
//
//  You can also edit on the page itself and use
//  "Copy readings.js" to get this whole file back, ready to paste.
//
//  These first readings are drafts written to be argued with.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

window.READINGS_AUTHOR = `Bakhshi`;

window.READINGS = {
  "The Presocratics on physis": `Everything later in this atlas is a quarrel over a word these men coined. Heraclitus’ fragment that nature loves to hide is the exact opposite of the Baconian programme, and I find it the wiser of the two: it makes concealment a property of nature, not a failure of our instruments. Democritus is the hinge, though; once colour exists only by convention, the knower has already begun to step out of the world.`,

  "Plato, Timaeus": `The phrase that stays with me is the heavenly plant. It reverses the direction of our roots: the human is anchored above and hangs down into the earth. Every later fight about whether we are part of nature or apart from it is already resolved here in a third way. We are part of nature because nature itself is ensouled, and we are apart from it in the same sense that the cosmos is apart from its own body.`,

  "Aristotle, Physics II": `Aristotle is the man the seventeenth century had to kill, and reading him again I see why they could not simply refute him. His nature is a principle inside things, and so it cannot be tortured, only frustrated. The acorn that sprouts beds is a joke against Plato’s craftsman, but it is also the deepest point in the atlas: nature is what things do when nobody is doing anything to them.`,

  "Lucretius, On the Nature of Things": `What surprises me each time is that the first mechanist in the West wrote a hymn. Lucretius proves that a nature without purpose need not be dead; it can be prodigal and erotic. The seventeenth century took his atoms and left his Venus behind, and the loss is part of what I am trying to trace.`,

  "The Stoics on nature and providence": `The Stoics give me the most comfortable cosmos in the atlas and I distrust it for that reason. A nature that is reason through and through can never be wronged, only misunderstood. The line in Balbus about a second nature made by human hands surprised me; the Baconian dream is already here, but under the condition that the whole is divine and we are its guests.`,

  "Plotinus, Ennead III.8, On Nature and Contemplation": `Nature producing by contemplating is the most beautiful idea in this section, and the most dangerous. If making is a lesser contemplation, then the technician is a failed mystic, and Plotinus would read Bacon as a man who could not sit still. But the same passage gives the occult tradition its theory: if the cosmos is one living web of sympathies, magic is simply knowing where to pull.`,

  "Laozi, Daodejing": `The straw dogs line is the most unsentimental sentence about nature in any tradition, and it belongs to the text most often read as a nature mysticism. What Laozi offers is not love of nature but deference to it. The sacred vessel in chapter 29 is the whole of my question in five characters: you cannot work on it without spoiling it.`,

  "Zhuangzi": `The machine heart is the sentence I would put on the front of this atlas if I were allowed only one. It names, two thousand years before Heidegger, the idea that a tool changes the user before it changes the world. Hundun’s seven holes are the best parable I know of what happens when nature is made to answer our senses.`,

  "The Mohists on heaven and the canons": `I included the Mohists because they spoil the stereotype that Chinese thought was uniformly organic and contemplative. Here is a measuring, engineering, utility-minded school with a personal Heaven behind it. That the tradition let them disappear tells us something, and it is not that the Chinese could not think like Bacon.`,

  "Xunzi, Discourse on Heaven": `Put Xunzi beside Bacon and the resemblance is startling: exalt heaven or domesticate it. But Xunzi’s mastery serves ritual order, not production, and his heaven is indifferent rather than reluctant. It does not need to be tortured because it is not hiding anything. That difference, more than any absence of method, may be what kept China from the Baconian path.`,

  "Huainanzi": `The seasonal ordinances are the most practical ecology I know before modern times: there are months when the axe must not enter the forest. They come wrapped in a cosmology that modern science rejects, which raises a question I cannot settle. Was the restraint possible only because the cosmology was false, or would a true cosmology also have required it?`,

  "Dong Zhongshu, Luxuriant Gems of the Spring and Autumn": `Dong Zhongshu makes nature into a moral messenger, and it is easy to laugh at the 366 joints. But his theory gave scholars a way to criticize emperors by reading floods as rebukes. A nature that speaks is politically useful to the weak. The disenchantment of nature also took that weapon away.`,

  "Zhang Zai, The Western Inscription": `The Western Inscription is short enough to memorize, and I think it does more for an ethics of nature than many modern books. It does not argue that nature has rights; it argues that nature is kin. The difficulty is the same as with any family: kinship tells you that you owe, not what you owe.`,

  "Zhu Xi on principle and vital stuff": `Zhu Xi is the closest Chinese parallel to the early moderns, an investigator of things with a unified theory, and yet the result is the opposite of Bacon. The principle he sought in things was also a norm, so investigation could never be value-free. Whether that is a weakness or the point is something I go back and forth on.`,

  "Wang Yangming and the bamboo": `I enjoy the bamboo story because it is the anti-Bacon told as a comedy. Two young men try to make nature confess by sheer attention and fall ill. Wang’s answer, that the principle is in the mind’s response, has its own danger, since it can collapse nature into feeling. But his list of what the mind grieves over, ending with tiles and stones, has never left me.`,

  "Wang Fuzhi on concrete things": `I placed Wang Fuzhi here because he is the strongest Chinese case of a nature that is historical. Human nature is produced daily is a sentence a pragmatist would sign. It makes him a natural partner for Marx later in the atlas, though he would have found Marx’s faith in production strangely thin.`,

  "Needham, Science and Civilisation in China": `Needham asks the question I came to this atlas with, from the other side. His answer, that Europe needed a divine legislator to imagine laws of nature, implies that the scientific revolution was theological, and that a world without a lawgiver can be orderly without being lawful. I am not sure he is right, but it is the most interesting wrong answer I know.`,

  "Rig Veda: ṛta and the cosmic person": `Two hymns sit side by side in the tenth book and pull against each other. One makes the cosmos a body and society its anatomy; the other ends with the most honest line in any scripture, that even the overseer may not know. I keep the second and distrust the first, because a nature that grounds caste is a nature too convenient for its interpreters.`,

  "The Upaniṣads on brahman and the world": `Food is brahman is the line I return to. It refuses to separate the sacred from the eaten, and it puts the discovery of the absolute through the stomach first. The Īśā’s enjoy by renouncing is the most compressed ecological instruction I know, and it is addressed not to the state but to the individual appetite.`,

  "Īśvarakṛṣṇa, Sāṃkhya Kārikā": `This is the most precise pre-modern anticipation of Cartesian dualism, and it inverts Descartes completely. Thinking belongs to nature; consciousness alone stands apart, and it does nothing. Descartes puts thought on the side of spirit and gives it dominion over matter. Sāṃkhya puts thought in matter and asks spirit only to stop being fascinated. I find the Sāṃkhya division more plausible after a century of neuroscience, which is not something I expected to write.`,

  "Patañjali, Yoga Sūtra": `Patañjali gives me the occult’s central temptation and its refusal in a single chapter. The powers over nature are real for him, earned by concentration rather than by machines, and he tells the yogi to walk past them. That is a discipline Crowley never accepted and Bacon never needed, since Bacon wanted the powers for everyone and the yogi wanted none of them.`,

  "Kaṇāda, Vaiśeṣika Sūtra": `Here is an atomism as rigorous as Democritus’ that never became a mechanism. The atoms move because of merit and demerit, so physics is woven into karma from the first instant. What strikes me is that the self in liberation becomes unconscious, a stone among stones. That is the strangest end in the Indian section, and an honest consequence of making consciousness a mere quality.`,

  "The Cārvāka materialists": `The fermenting-ingredients analogy for consciousness is exactly the emergence argument that philosophers of mind still debate. That it survives only in the mouths of its enemies is a reminder that Indian thought, so often sold as uniformly spiritual, contained its own Hobbes. I would give much for one complete Cārvāka book.`,

  "Jainism: life in the elements": `The opening of the Ācārāṅga, with its catalogue of the lives one harms by kindling fire or digging earth, is the only text in the atlas that makes every human act a wound to nature. It is unlivable in full, and that is its strength: it makes the cost visible. The objection that it serves self-purification does not trouble me as much as it troubles the critics.`,

  "The Buddha on dependent origination": `Interdependence has become an ecological slogan, and the slogan takes half of the doctrine. For the Buddha, seeing that everything is conditioned is the beginning of release from it, not a reason to love it. Still, the forest verses show monks who were moved by rain and peacocks; release and delight seem to have lived together more easily than the theory suggests.`,

  "Nāgārjuna, Fundamental Verses on the Middle Way": `Nāgārjuna is in this atlas because he denies the premise of the governing question. There is no nature to ask about, in the sense of a <i>physis</i> that things have. His identity of <i>saṃsāra</i> and <i>nirvāṇa</i> is the opposite of Sāṃkhya’s escape and might be the only Indian position from which an ecology without renunciation follows.`,

  "Caraka Saṃhitā: the person equal to the world": `The Āyurvedic sentence that the person equals the world is the Indian version of the microcosm, and unlike most versions it is a working principle for treating people. The epidemic chapter, where land, water and air go bad together through human fault, reads uncannily now. I do not need the humours to find the structure of the thought true.`,

  "Śaṅkara on māyā": `Māyā is easy to mistake for a contempt of nature, and Śaṅkara’s defenders are right that he grants the world full empirical reality. But the snake-and-rope image does decide one thing: nothing done to the world touches what is real. It is the purest form of the ‘apart from it’ stance in the atlas, purer than Descartes, and like Descartes it leaves nature with no claim on us.`,

  "Abhinavagupta and the Tantric cosmos": `Abhinavagupta gives India a living, vibrating cosmos that does not need to be renounced, and it is no accident that Western occultists from Woodroffe onward found Tantra so attractive. The difference I notice is that here power over the world is recognized as one’s own nature, not seized from outside. That is either the deepest critique of Bacon or his mystical twin, and the entries in the occult section have made me unsure which.`,

  "J. C. Bose, Response in the Living and Non-Living": `Bose is the one figure in this atlas who tried to use the Baconian instrument to prove an Upaniṣadic conclusion. The establishment’s irritation was partly scientific and partly that a colonial subject was blurring the very line their method relied on. His failure on metals does not undo the question he forced: if the instrument shows continuity, which side gives way?`,

  "Tagore, Sādhanā": `Tagore’s city wall is an unforgettable image, and I believe it is false as history and true as diagnosis. Greece had its sacred groves, and India burned its forests for farmland. But the habit of seeing nature as something outside the wall describes the modern West well, and Tagore said it in 1913, before the ecologists.`,

  "Sri Aurobindo, The Life Divine": `Aurobindo is Teilhard’s Indian twin, and both make evolution into a spiritual ascent. What I value is the chapter on the two negations, which rejects asceticism as firmly as materialism; it is rare for an Indian spiritual writer to say that renunciation was a mistake. What I resist is the confidence that the direction of nature is known.`,

  "Gandhi, Hind Swaraj": `Hind Swaraj is the political consequence of Zhuangzi’s gardener. Gandhi refuses the machine because it remakes the man, and he made that refusal into a mass movement for a few years. Nehru won the argument about India’s future, and the earth’s present condition has reopened it, though I am not sure Gandhi’s village answers the question it raises.`,

  "The Qurʾān on signs and the trust": `The verse of the trust is where I begin whenever I think about nature as a Muslim. The heavens and mountains refused what we accepted, and the verse’s own verdict on us is unjust and ignorant. The same scripture that subjects nature to us also says that nature was wiser than we were in refusing power. That is not a charter for mastery; it is a warning attached to it.`,

  "Ikhwān al-Ṣafāʾ, The Case of the Animals versus Man": `Nothing in the medieval world is like this book. The animals have the better arguments for almost the whole trial, and the humans win only by pointing to the few among them who are saints. The judgement is a rebuke: you are permitted to rule only to the extent that you are not like yourselves. I would make every student of engineering read it.`,

  "Ibn Sīnā on nature and the flying man": `The flying man is Descartes’ cogito six centuries early, and the comparison clarifies both. Ibn Sīnā’s self knows itself apart from the body, but it does not thereby become the master of an inert extended world; nature keeps its forms and its ends. That tells me the Cartesian split alone did not produce the modern attitude. Something else had to die along with final causes.`,

  "Al-Ghazālī, The Incoherence of the Philosophers, Discussion 17": `Al-Ghazālī is blamed in Pakistan, in every lazy essay on decline, for killing Muslim science. Discussion 17 says nothing of the kind. It says that observation finds regularity and not necessity, which Hume says too. What he removes from nature is power, not order, and a nature without power of its own is a strange fit with the later fantasy of conquering it.`,

  "Ibn Rushd, The Incoherence of the Incoherence": `Ibn Rushd’s point that a thing without powers has no definition is unanswerable as logic, and the moderns, who denied powers to matter, ended up with things defined only by geometry. In that sense the seventeenth century took al-Ghazālī’s side without knowing it, and gave the powers to laws instead of to God.`,

  "Ibn Ṭufayl, Ḥayy ibn Yaqẓān": `Ḥayy’s rules for eating are the most carefully reasoned ecological ethic in the medieval world, and he derives them from cosmology, not from sentiment. He takes the least from each species and plants its seeds again. That a twelfth-century Andalusian physician thought this was what imitating the heavens required tells me that dominion was never the only reading available.`,

  "Ibn ʿArabī, The Ringstones of Wisdom": `Ibn ʿArabī makes the human being both the summit of nature and its servant: the polish of the mirror exists for the sake of the reflection, not for itself. The Perfect Human gathers all the names, which looks like dominion, yet he is defined as the one who gives each thing its due. Iqbal resisted this whole tradition, and I think he gave it too little credit.`,

  "Rūmī, I died as a mineral": `These six lines are quoted by everyone, from Iqbal to evolution enthusiasts, and I think each side is half right. Rūmī is not anticipating natural selection, but he is saying that the human being is continuous with mineral, plant and animal, and that this is a cause for hope rather than humiliation. Darwin delivered the continuity without the hope.`,

  "Mullā Ṣadrā on substantial motion": `Mullā Ṣadrā and Descartes are exact contemporaries, and I cannot think of a better pair for showing that the seventeenth century had two roads. One split soul from body and froze matter into extension. The other made the soul grow out of the body and set all matter in motion toward spirit. We took the first road, and Ṣadrā was barely read outside Iran until the twentieth century.`,

  "Sayyid Ahmad Khan on the work and the word of God": `The word <i>nechar</i> entered Urdu as an insult, which tells me how foreign the idea of a self-standing nature was. Sir Syed’s solution, that the work cannot contradict the word, is generous and dangerous at once: it gives modern science the casting vote in interpretation. Aligarh did immense good, and I think this principle was where it went wrong, since it imported Victorian physics as if it were the work of God itself.`,

  "Iqbal, The Reconstruction of Religious Thought in Islam": `Iqbal is the figure in this atlas I argue with most, because his poetry is woven into the culture I belong to. He turns nature into God’s habit and then tells the self to conquer it, and I do not think the two halves fit. If nature is God’s character, conquering it is a strange form of devotion. His claim that Muslims originated the inductive method also leaves untouched the question of whether the method’s spirit of conquest is anything to boast about.`,

  "Genesis and Job: dominion and the whirlwind": `I read Genesis 1 and Job 38 together because they correct each other. The first gives us rule over the creatures, the second asks whether we can bind the Pleiades. If Bacon read only the first, the whirlwind is the reply he never heard. The pairing in the Hebrew of till and keep, which also means serve and guard, is a reading I find more persuasive than the reading of dominion as licence.`,

  "Augustine, The Literal Meaning of Genesis": `Augustine’s two orders, nature and use, are the most useful distinction I found in the medieval material. The mouse outranks the gem in being, and we prefer the gem anyway. He saw that the hierarchy of value and the hierarchy of use come apart, and the modern economy is what happens when the second swallows the first.`,

  "Maimonides, The Guide of the Perplexed III.13": `Here, in 1190, is a rabbi telling his reader that the universe was not made for him. Maimonides sees what Lynn White missed: that the biblical tradition contains a powerful internal critique of anthropocentrism. Each species for its own sake is a sentence I wish the economists had read.`,

  "Hildegard of Bingen, The Book of Divine Works": `The elements crying out against human sin is a medieval image I have no wish to correct. Hildegard puts the human at the centre of the wheel, the posture later made famous by Leonardo, but surrounded by winds and animals that press in. The centre is the most exposed place in her cosmos, not the most powerful.`,

  "Francis of Assisi, The Canticle of the Creatures": `The ambiguity in the Italian, praise God per these creatures, says everything. It can mean by them, as instruments, or for them, as gifts, or through them, as companions. I take the third. Francis is the only figure in the atlas who addresses fire as a brother, and I do not think we have learned anything since that makes him wrong.`,

  "Bonaventure and the book of creatures": `The book of nature is a medieval image that the moderns kept and inverted. Galileo said it was written in mathematics; Bacon said it should be read for power. Bonaventure’s book was written for the reader’s conversion. What changed was not that nature stopped being a book but what reading it was for.`,

  "Aquinas on dominion and natural law": `Aquinas gives nature real natures and real ends, as Aristotle did, and then orders the whole to humans. The two moves are in tension: a thing with its own end cannot simply be for us. His argument about cruelty, that it trains a man to be cruel, is psychologically acute and morally inadequate. Kant repeated it, and I think both of them knew better.`,

  "Nicholas of Cusa, On Learned Ignorance": `Cusa’s decentred universe arrives a century before Copernicus and has the opposite emotional tone from Pascal’s terror at infinite spaces. For him an infinite universe is a cause for wonder and the earth is ennobled by losing the centre. I wonder how much of modern anxiety came not from the cosmology but from losing the God who made the decentring joyful.`,

  "Ficino, Three Books on Life": `Ficino’s farmer is the gentlest image of human power over nature in this atlas, and it comes from the magical tradition, not the scientific one. The magus prepares and marries; he does not wrestle or vex. If there was a turn in the West from cooperation to conquest, it happened between Ficino’s farmer and Bacon’s Proteus, a little more than a century apart.`,

  "Pico della Mirandola, Oration on the Dignity of Man": `The speech to Adam is the birth certificate of the self-made human, and its implication for nature is that we alone are outside it. A being without a fixed nature stands over every being that has one. Pico’s magic is still a marriage, but his Adam has already left the house.`,

  "Paracelsus and the light of nature": `Paracelsus is the hinge between the magician and the experimenter, and his rule that one must walk the book of nature with one’s feet is more Baconian than anything in Bacon. The alchemist as baker, finishing what nature began, also contains the idea of perfecting nature that the moderns turned into improving it. The signatures were wrong. The posture of humble experience was not.`,

  "Telesio, On the Nature of Things according to Their Own Principles": `Telesio deserves to be better known for one phrase, according to their own principles. It names the first step every modern took. But his nature senses and seeks its own preservation, so the step away from Aristotle did not yet lead to a dead nature. That required another fifty years.`,

  "Bruno, Cause, Principle and Unity and the infinite universe": `Bruno’s inner artist is the strongest Renaissance alternative to the mechanical philosophy: an infinite universe that is alive from within. The Inquisition’s fire has made him a martyr for science, which he was not, and Yates made him a magician, which is closer. I read him as the man who saw that an infinite universe needs an infinite life, and was right to fear a universe that had only infinite extension.`,

  "Campanella, On the Sense of Things and on Magic": `Campanella defended Galileo from prison while holding that stones feel. That combination is impossible for us and was natural for him. His City of the Sun is also an early technocracy in which astrology governs breeding, a warning that a living nature does not by itself make for a gentle politics.`,

  "Montaigne, Apology for Raymond Sebond": `Montaigne’s cat is the last word of the Renaissance and the first target of the moderns. Descartes wrote the beast-machine doctrine partly in answer to this essay. I think the cat has the better of the argument, and Montaigne’s confession that he cannot watch a chicken killed is, to me, a more reliable guide than Descartes’ certainty that the dog does not feel.`,

  "Bacon, The New Organon": `Reading the aphorisms again, what strikes me is how much humility is mixed into the arrogance. The human mind is an enchanted glass, nature is subtler than we are, and we conquer only by obeying. But obedience here is tactical. One obeys the way a general studies terrain. The theological framing, the recovery of a dominion lost in Eden, makes the conquest a restoration, and that is what allowed it to seem innocent for three centuries.`,

  "Bacon on vexing nature, and the New Atlantis": `So Bacon did not write that we must torture nature, and the misquotation has had a long life because it states a truth the texts make in milder words. Vex, constrain, squeeze, mould, bind to service, make a slave: that is a vocabulary of force even if no rack appears. The Salomon’s House sentence, the effecting of all things possible, is the one I find truly frightening. It has no limit in it. Every later critique in this atlas is an argument about that missing limit.`,

  "Galileo, The Assayer": `The tickling feather is, to my mind, the most consequential example in modern philosophy. With it Galileo moves colour, sound, warmth and taste out of the world into the mind, and the world left behind is the one physics studies. Everything we now call the hard problem of consciousness is the bill for that move. Whitehead and Husserl saw it; neuroscience still pays it.`,

  "Kepler, The Harmony of the World": `Kepler is the man the twenty-first century can least imagine: the discoverer of exact laws who believed the earth had a soul and wrote, in the same years, that the heavens are a clock. He shows me that the mechanical philosophy did not arrive all at once. It grew inside minds that still held the older picture, and took over without anyone deciding that it should.`,

  "Descartes, Discourse on the Method": `As it were, masters and possessors: the qualifier <i>comme</i> is easy to drop, and readers have dropped it for four hundred years. Descartes is more careful than his reputation, and medicine, not domination, is his first goal. But the two tests in Part V, language and versatility, are where the denial of feeling to animals rests, and they are also, almost word for word, the tests we now apply to machines that talk. He drew a line that today runs through the middle of the machines themselves.`,

  "Descartes, Meditations and the Treatise on Man": `The fountains at Saint-Germain are where the modern picture of the human was born: a body of pipes and a ghost who sits at the valves. Elisabeth’s letter is still the best objection, and Descartes’ reply, that the union is something we experience but cannot understand, is honest. The pilot-in-a-ship passage shows that he knew his split was too clean. His followers, not Descartes himself, made it into a doctrine.`,

  "Hobbes, Leviathan and De Corpore": `Hobbes is the honest Cartesian: if animals are machines and the body is a machine, then so is the mind. His opening sentence makes the state the second product of the same art that makes clocks. Read for nature, <i>Leviathan</i> is a theory of why human nature must be overruled. Nature is not a mother here but a war, and the sovereign is how we escape it.`,

  "Gassendi, the Fifth Objections and the revival of Epicurus": `The exchange of insults is the best moment in the Objections. Gassendi’s ‘O Mind’ catches the absurdity of a thinking thing without a body, and Descartes’ ‘O Flesh’ catches the confusion of a body that thinks. Neither won, and we have been repeating the exchange since. Gassendi shows that the new physics could be Epicurean without becoming godless, which is how it entered Christian Europe.`,

  "Boyle, A Free Enquiry into the Vulgarly Received Notion of Nature": `Boyle is the figure I most underestimated before this atlas. Bacon wanted to conquer nature; Boyle wanted to abolish the word. A pious man, he removed nature’s agency to keep God’s honour safe, and he is the clearest case of my suspicion that the disenchantment of nature was carried out for theological reasons. A clock cannot be wronged, and so it can be used without limit.`,

  "Newton, the Principia and the Queries": `The man who completed the mechanical world-picture thought gravity at a distance was absurd and kept active principles in his Queries. Newton was more like Kepler than his followers were like Newton. The general scholium is a hymn, and the queries are an alchemist’s notebook made public.`,

  "Margaret Cavendish, Observations upon Experimental Philosophy": `Cavendish says the thing no one else in the seventeenth century would say: that an experiment tells you about the experiment. The microscope shows you a flea distorted by glass, and Hooke has drawn a picture of his instrument. She was wrong about what instruments could do and right about what they changed. A nature that perceives, in every part, is the first answer to Descartes that I find myself defending.`,

  "Anne Conway, The Principles of the Most Ancient and Modern Philosophy": `Conway solved Elisabeth’s problem by denying its premise. If body is condensed spirit, then spirit can move body because they were never two things. It is a cleaner answer than Descartes gave, and it cost her the inert matter that physics wanted. She wrote it in pain and in seclusion, and it has the calm of someone who refused to separate her mind from her suffering body.`,

  "More and Cudworth on plastic nature": `Cudworth’s plastic nature is a theologian’s Aristotle, an unconscious craft that lets God rest. Bayle’s objection is brilliant: if nature can work artfully without knowing, you no longer need a mind behind it, which is exactly Darwin’s point two centuries later. The defence of a living nature turned out to supply the atheists with their best argument.`,

  "Spinoza, Ethics": `Spinoza is the only seventeenth-century thinker who refused to set the human apart from nature, and he did it more rigorously than any ecologist since. Then, in one scholium, he permits us to use animals as we please. The lesson I draw is that dissolving the human into nature does not by itself produce care. Spinoza’s nature includes the wolf’s appetite as well as the lamb’s, and ours.`,

  "Pascal, Pensées": `Pascal is the first writer to feel the mechanical universe as a human being feels it. Cusa’s infinite gave joy; Pascal’s terrifies. The thinking reed makes dignity lie entirely in knowing, and so it sets the human against a universe that does not know. I find this the most honest account of what Descartes’ world feels like from inside.`,

  "Malebranche, The Search after Truth": `Malebranche is al-Ghazālī in Paris. Whether he read the Arabic tradition is unclear, but his argument is the same: no necessary connection in nature, so only God acts. The difference is that he joined it to the beast-machine, and the anecdote of the kicked dog, true or not, shows what the combination makes possible. Al-Ghazālī did not have a machine to put in the dog.`,

  "Leibniz, Monadology and Nature Itself": `The mill argument still stands as the best statement of the hard problem: walk through a thinking machine and you find only gears. Leibniz’s answer, that perception goes all the way down, makes him the ancestor of today’s panpsychists. His garden within the garden is also the most beautiful image of nature in the century, and it comes from the man who invented the calculus.`,

  "Locke on qualities, thinking matter and property": `Locke closes this section because he joins the two halves of the century’s settlement. Secondary qualities move value-bearing experience into the mind, and the property chapter makes value come from labour. Nature unworked is waste. Put the two together and nature in itself has neither colour nor worth, and this is the frame in which we still think. The America example shows who paid for it first.`,

  "La Mettrie, Man a Machine": `La Mettrie completes Descartes by refusing his exception, and he draws a conclusion I did not expect: that we should be kind. If the human is a machine like the animal, then the animal is a person like us. The beast-machine and the machine-man look identical but run in opposite moral directions, and La Mettrie took the generous one.`,

  "Hume, Enquiry and Dialogues Concerning Natural Religion": `Hume did to the mechanical philosophy what al-Ghazālī did to Aristotle: he removed necessity and left custom. Philo’s suggestion that the world is more like a vegetable than a machine is a joke with consequences. If generation is as good an explanation as design, Darwin has his opening, and the clockmaker becomes one hypothesis among several.`,

  "Rousseau, the natural man and the Reveries": `Rousseau gives pity to animals because they feel, not because they reason. That moves the line between us and them from thought to suffering, where Bentham later put it. The Fifth Walk, floating in a boat on the lake and feeling only one’s own existence, is a description of being inside nature that no Cartesian could write.`,

  "Kant, Critique of Judgment": `Kant restates Bacon’s interrogation in the language of a courtroom: the judge compels witnesses. It is the most exact version of the image Merchant attributed to Bacon, and it is in Kant. Then the same book says no Newton will explain a blade of grass. Kant held both, the interrogator and the man humbled by grass, and I think that is the honest modern position before Darwin.`,

  "Goethe, Metamorphosis of Plants and Theory of Colours": `The man as the most exact apparatus is Goethe’s answer to Galileo and to Cavendish’s microscope at once. He lost the argument about light. He did not lose the argument about what kind of knower one becomes by studying nature. A delicate empiricism that changes the observer is, for me, the most precise opposite of vexing nature in the whole atlas.`,

  "Schelling, Ideas for a Philosophy of Nature": `Visible spirit and invisible nature is the Romantic reply to Descartes in seven words. It denies the split by making mind nature’s late flowering. I find the formula more convincing than any of Schelling’s physics, and it returns, without credit, in the emergentists and the panpsychists of our own day.`,

  "Blake against single vision": `Blake saw that Bacon, Newton and Locke were a single position, decades before historians did. His Newton bent over his compass at the bottom of the sea is the best image I know of the knower who has stepped out of nature to measure it and has stopped seeing it. Single vision is exactly what Galileo’s feather made into method.`,

  "Wordsworth and Coleridge": `We murder to dissect is the Romantic answer to the anatomists and, I think, an unfair one. But the line from ‘Tintern Abbey’ about a motion and a spirit that rolls through all things is Stoic physics in blank verse, and it reminds me that the older picture of a living cosmos never died; it moved from philosophy into poetry, where scientists were not obliged to answer it.`,

  "Humboldt, Cosmos": `Humboldt is the man who should have been the hinge of modern science and became a statue in a hundred parks. He combined measurement with feeling and saw the climatic effect of deforestation in 1800. That his synthesis was abandoned for specialization says more about the institutions than the idea.`,

  "Emerson and Thoreau": `Emerson’s nature is still a book, as in Bonaventure, but the reader has become the author. Thoreau goes further and makes wildness a value, not a symbol. His flowering records, now used to measure warming, are the strangest fact in this atlas: a Romantic’s diary has become scientific evidence of the damage his age began.`,

  "Darwin, The Origin of Species and The Descent of Man": `Darwin finished the job Descartes started and reversed its meaning. Descartes put the human outside nature; Darwin put the human back in, and made the mind of the dog continuous with ours. The tangled bank at the end of the <i>Origin</i> is the best one-paragraph description of an ecology ever written. That his theory was used for conquest is a fact about his readers.`,

  "Mill, Nature": `Mill is the most honest opponent of nature in the atlas, and the essay is a relief after so much reverence. He is right that nature is no moral teacher. He is also the man who, in the same decade, hoped for a stationary state with wild flowers in it. The two together are exactly my position on most days: nature is not good, and we need it anyway.`,

  "Marx on metabolism with nature": `Engels’ sentence about nature’s revenge for every victory, written in 1876, is the most prophetic in this section. Marx’s metabolism is a better word for our relation to nature than either stewardship or mastery, because it is both necessary and capable of breaking. Whether the productive forces he trusted can ever be compatible with it is the question that the twentieth century answered badly.`,

  "Nietzsche on the de-deification of nature": `Nietzsche denies both pictures, the organism and the machine, which nobody else in the century does. He wants nature naturalized all the way, without God and without morality. Yet in the same decade he calls our attitude to nature hubris. I take that contradiction as the most important clue in the modern section: even a man who strips nature of all meaning still recoils from what we are doing to it.`,

  "The Corpus Hermeticum and the Asclepius": `The Hermetic Man falls in love with nature as Narcissus with his reflection, and the union is not a fall but a marriage. This is the opposite of Bacon’s Proteus and also its source. The great miracle that can make gods is the first statement of a human power over nature that operates by knowledge alone, and the Renaissance took it as a licence.`,

  "The Emerald Tablet": `As above, so below is the most quoted occult formula and the least understood. It is not a claim of mastery but of correspondence: the adept works gently, separating with great skill. That Newton translated it in his own hand is the fact that most unsettles the tidy history of science I was taught.`,

  "Iamblichus, On the Mysteries": `Iamblichus draws the line that later occultism forgot. Theurgy does not compel; it prepares. The divine comes down because the soul has made itself a fitting place, not because it has been commanded. That is the difference between a rite and a technique, and it is the difference between Iamblichus and Crowley.`,

  "The Jābirian corpus and the science of the balance": `The Jābirian <i>takwīn</i> is the most extreme ambition in the medieval world: to make a human being in a flask by getting the numbers right. It shows that the dream of total control over nature did not wait for Bacon. It arose inside a cosmology of qualities and letters, and only the cosmology changed when modern chemistry arrived.`,

  "Picatrix (Ghāyat al-Ḥakīm)": `The Picatrix taught me that the occult tradition was never purely contemplative. It is a manual for effects, and its view of the human as a lesser world is exactly what licenses the operator to work on the greater. Bacon despised such books and inherited their ambition.`,

  "Kabbalah: the Zohar and Luria": `<i>Tiqqun</i> is the most hopeful idea about human action in nature in the whole occult section. The world is broken, and we repair it not by mastering it but by raising what is holy within it. It gives humans an enormous role without making them masters, and I think it is the best answer to the choice between stewardship and conquest that I have found.`,

  "Chinese inner alchemy": `The Chinese alchemists also went against nature, and the comparison with Bacon is precise. Bacon reverses nature to produce works; the adept reverses it to return to the One. Both say that nature left to itself leads where we do not want to go. Only one of them makes the remedy an industry.`,

  "Agrippa, Three Books of Occult Philosophy": `Agrippa is the clearest case of the Renaissance magus as the scientist’s ancestor. His magic is the perfection of natural philosophy, it requires mathematics, and it aims at operations. The fact that he also wrote a book against all knowledge, including his own, is the most human detail in the occult section. The magus doubted himself; the engineer seldom does.`,

  "John Dee, the Monas Hieroglyphica and the angelic conversations": `Dee is the same man in two roles that we now separate: the promoter of applied mathematics and empire, and the man asking angels for the language of creation. I think it was one project, the recovery of Adam’s knowledge and power. Bacon wanted the same thing and dropped the angels.`,

  "The Rosicrucian manifestos": `The Rosicrucians promised a universal reformation through hidden knowledge of nature, and six years later Bacon published the Great Instauration with the same promise made public. Yates argued that the two belonged together. Whether or not she was right, the manifestos show how much of the scientific dream was first dreamed as a secret society.`,

  "Jacob Boehme, The Signature of All Things": `A shoemaker saw a pewter dish in the sun and wrote that nature has a mouth. I have no idea whether the unground means anything, but the claim that every thing expresses its inner nature outwardly is one that Goethe, Schelling and the phenomenologists all inherited. Boehme is the occult tradition’s gift to German philosophy.`,

  "Robert Fludd and his quarrels": `The quarrel between Fludd and Kepler is the hinge of the century. Both believed in cosmic harmony. Kepler wanted it measured and Fludd wanted it contemplated. The weapon-salve looks absurd, until one remembers that Newton’s gravity was also action at a distance, and that Newton himself thought it absurd.`,

  "Newton the alchemist": `Keynes’ phrase, the last of the magicians, is too good to be quite right, and Newman’s correction is persuasive. But the ‘Vegetation’ manuscript is still astonishing: a distinction between mechanical chemistry and a living fire in matter, written by the man whose mechanics would make that fire unnecessary. Newton did not believe in the world his followers built on him.`,

  "Swedenborg, Heaven and Hell": `Swedenborg is the scientist who turned seer, and his doctrine of correspondences gave Blake, Emerson and Baudelaire their idea of nature as a language. I find the details unreadable and the principle haunting: the natural world exists so that the spiritual can take shape. It is the exact inverse of Galileo, for whom the qualities were the mind’s and the world was geometry.`,

  "Mesmer and animal magnetism": `The commission’s verdict, that the effects came from imagination, is where scientific medicine discovered the placebo, and it did so by testing a theory of cosmic fluid. Mesmer was wrong about nature and right that the mind acts on the body in ways the mechanists could not account for. Hypnosis and psychotherapy are his unacknowledged heirs.`,

  "Éliphas Lévi, Dogma and Ritual of High Magic": `Lévi is where the occult becomes modern, which means where it becomes about will. The astral light is Mesmer’s fluid with a kabbalistic name, and the magus is the man who commands it. From here the line to Crowley is direct. The older magic of correspondence and receptivity is quietly replaced by a magic of command.`,

  "Blavatsky, The Secret Doctrine": `Blavatsky is a mixture of brilliance, borrowing and fraud, and I cannot leave her out because her influence was enormous, including on Indian and Sri Lankan nationalism. Her nature is evolutionary, alive and spiritual, a Darwin re-enchanted. The root races show how quickly a spiritualized evolution becomes a hierarchy of peoples.`,

  "The Hermetic Order of the Golden Dawn": `The Golden Dawn turned the living cosmos of the Renaissance into a filing system. Everything corresponds to something on a table, and the initiate learns the table. Yeats rescued something larger from it, the great memory of Nature, which is close to what Jung would call the collective unconscious.`,

  "Aleister Crowley, The Book of the Law and Magick": `Crowley is in this atlas because his definition of magick is Bacon’s programme with the theology removed. Change in conformity with will, and every intentional act is magical: this is the engineer’s creed in a black robe. I find it clarifying rather than shocking. It shows that the difference between occultism and technology was never about nature; it was about whose will is served.`,

  "Dion Fortune and magic as change in consciousness": `Fortune’s small change to Crowley’s definition, consciousness instead of nature, marks a retreat that I find honest. The modern occultist conceded the outer world to science and kept the inner. It is the same division as Descartes’, made from the other side.`,

  "Rudolf Steiner and the Agriculture Course": `Steiner is easy to mock, and biodynamic farming is the most practical occult legacy in the world: wine estates and organic farms use it. The idea of the farm as an organism with its own individuality is, scientific claims aside, a better starting point for agriculture than the factory model. The cow horn I leave to the reader.`,

  "Gurdjieff and Ouspensky: the ray of creation": `Gurdjieff’s ‘man is a machine’ is La Mettrie’s sentence turned into a diagnosis. For La Mettrie it was a fact to accept; for Gurdjieff it is a sleep to be woken from. The thought that a soul is not given but made is, to me, the most interesting idea in this whole stretch of the occult revival.`,

  "Jung, Psychology and Alchemy": `Jung rescued alchemy by moving it indoors, into the psyche, and in doing so he accepted the Cartesian division he claimed to heal. The <i>unus mundus</i> is a genuine attempt to rejoin mind and matter, worked out with the physicist Pauli. Principe and Newman have shown that the alchemists were doing chemistry; Jung showed what they were dreaming while they did it.`,

  "Gerald Gardner and Wicca": `Wicca is the occult tradition’s return to a living, sacred nature after a century of magic as will. Its history was invented, and Hutton’s book is a model of how to say so without contempt. A religion of the turning year is, whatever its origins, the most direct answer to Boyle’s wish to abolish the goddess Nature: here she is again.`,

  "Austin Osman Spare and chaos magic": `Chaos magic is where the occult tradition ends up when it follows the logic of will to the bottom: nothing is true, every system is a tool, and the self is a costume. I place it here because it is the occult’s mirror of late capitalism, in which everything is a means. It is the most honest version of Crowley, and I find it bleak.`,

  "Frances Yates and the Hermetic thesis": `Yates is the right figure to close the occult section because she asks the question this atlas asks: where did the will to operate on nature come from? Her answer, the magus, is too simple, and her critics have shown the scientists rejecting magic in so many words. But they rejected the magic and kept the ambition, and I think that is all Yates needed to be right about.`,

  "Guénon, The Reign of Quantity": `Guénon’s phrase the reign of quantity names exactly what Galileo’s feather did. He sees the move to primary qualities not as a method but as a metaphysical choice, which I think is right. Where he loses me is the eschatology: if modern science is a sign of the end of the cycle, there is nothing to do but wait, and that does not seem to me what tradition asks of anyone.`,

  "Coomaraswamy, art imitates nature in her manner of operation": `Coomaraswamy’s maxim, art imitates nature in her manner of operation, is the best definition of a non-Baconian technology I know. Imitating how nature works is very different from forcing it to do what we want. The trouble, as with Gandhi, is that his model craftsman lives in a social order I cannot endorse.`,

  "Schuon on virgin nature": `Schuon gives the cleanest statement that nature is a sanctuary. I am moved by it and suspicious of it in equal measure: a temple not made by hands is a beautiful idea, and also one that turns real people, the Plains Indians in his case, into ornaments of a European’s vision.`,

  "Burckhardt, Alchemy and traditional cosmology": `Burckhardt and Jung read alchemy the same way, one through metaphysics and the other through psychology, and the historians reject both. I put him in because his work on Fez shows Traditionalism as a practice of conservation, not only a doctrine. A man who restores a medieval city has earned the right to criticize modern planning.`,

  "Julius Evola, Revolt Against the Modern World": `I include Evola with a warning on the door. He shows that a critique of modern science and a sacred view of nature can be joined to a politics of domination and race. The critique of Bacon does not by itself guarantee anything good. I find it useful to know that the road away from mechanism can lead here too.`,

  "Nasr, Man and Nature": `Nasr’s distinction between pontifical and Promethean man is, for me, the most useful pair of terms in the perennialist section. It names the two stances in the governing question, standing within nature as a bridge or over it as a master. But his prostitute image for modern nature is one I would cut, and I am not persuaded that the cure for the crisis is a return to medieval cosmology rather than a new humility within modern knowledge.`,

  "Huxley, The Perennial Philosophy": `Huxley’s reducing valve is the most interesting idea about consciousness in this section, the reverse of the emergence story: the brain does not produce mind, it filters it. His vase of flowers is also a reminder that the Romantic and perennialist vision of a living nature has always had a pharmacology, from soma to mescaline. I take his 1963 essay on ecology more seriously than his mysticism.`,

  "Weber, Science as a Vocation": `Weber’s definition is the most precise in the atlas: disenchantment is not knowing everything but believing that everything could be calculated. That belief, not any discovery, is what changed. Josephson-Storm’s objection is fair, and the occult section of this atlas proves his point. But a belief can rule a civilization’s institutions even while seances fill its parlours.`,

  "Husserl, The Crisis of European Sciences": `Husserl turns Galileo’s feather into a philosophical indictment, and his phrase the garb of ideas is exact: the formulas were a garment that we mistook for the body. The concealing genius is the right description. Galileo hid the lifeworld so well that it took three centuries and a refugee philosopher to notice it was missing.`,

  "Whitehead, The Concept of Nature and Science and the Modern World": `The bifurcation of nature is the name for what Galileo did, and the fallacy of misplaced concreteness is the name for what we did after him: mistaking the map for the territory. Whitehead’s process philosophy has become the shared ancestor of process theology, Needham’s organic view of China, and several of the new materialisms, which suggests he found the right problem.`,

  "Heidegger, The Question Concerning Technology": `The Rhine as a water-power supplier is the image that sums up this atlas’s fourth clause: what may we do to nature? Heidegger’s answer, that we no longer ask, is the deepest diagnosis I know. But I cannot read the essay without remembering the man’s politics, and it makes me wary of the nostalgia for the peasant and the wooden bridge.`,

  "Merleau-Ponty on the flesh of the world": `Merleau-Ponty answers Descartes with the experience of one hand touching the other: I am at once touching and touched, subject and object. It is the simplest refutation of the ghost in the machine I know, and it makes the human a fold in nature rather than a visitor. I wish he had lived to finish the lectures on nature.`,

  "Horkheimer and Adorno, Dialectic of Enlightenment": `The book opens with Bacon and closes the circle Bacon opened: knowledge as power over nature becomes power over people. The Odysseus chapter adds the piece most often missed, that dominating nature outside requires dominating the nature inside. The engineer who tortures nature first ties himself to the mast.`,

  "C. S. Lewis, The Abolition of Man": `Lewis gave me the thesis that runs through the occult section: magic and science were twins. And his argument that the conquest of nature is really the conquest of some men over others, with nature as the instrument, is the political truth of Baconianism. He wrote it for schoolteachers in 1943, and it reads now as a commentary on genetic engineering and on algorithms that shape what people want.`,

  "Teilhard de Chardin, The Phenomenon of Man": `Teilhard and Aurobindo, writing independently, both made evolution into the ascent of spirit, and both welcomed technology as part of it. His noosphere is often called a prophecy of the internet, which is not entirely a compliment. The within of things is the idea of his I keep, against Medawar.`,

  "Rachel Carson, Silent Spring": `Silent Spring is in this atlas because its last pages answer Bacon by name, almost: control of nature is arrogance. Carson does it with the instruments of the Baconian science itself, sampling, measuring, documenting. That is the pattern of the twentieth century I find most hopeful: science used to reveal what science made.`,

  "Hans Jonas, The Imperative of Responsibility": `Jonas is the man who took Bacon’s missing limit seriously and tried to write one. The effecting of all things possible meets the imperative of responsibility. His insight that traditional ethics never needed to think about the far future, because our power did not reach it, explains why every tradition in this atlas is silent on what we now most need to know.`,

  "Lynn White Jr., The Historical Roots of Our Ecologic Crisis": `White’s essay is four pages long and created a whole field. It is wrong in its simplicity and right in its question: what we do to nature depends on what we think we are. I placed Genesis and Job at the start of the Christendom section partly to answer him; the tradition he blamed contains its own refutation of him.`,

  "Carolyn Merchant, The Death of Nature": `The torture quotation that started this atlas came to me through Merchant’s book, and I now know it is not in Bacon. Yet her central claim survives the correction: the images we use for nature license what we do to it. The debate between her and Pesic is the right place to stand to look back at everything before it.`,

  "Arne Naess and deep ecology": `Naess is Spinoza with a rucksack, and his ecological Self is the most direct translation of the Upaniṣads into modern ethics. Guha’s critique has stayed with me more than the original, because it comes from the subcontinent: a deep ecology that removes villagers to save tigers has decided whose vital needs count.`,

  "Lovelock and Margulis, Gaia": `Gaia is the scientific return of the world-soul, under the strict condition that it has no intentions. Margulis’s refusal to call it an organism is, to me, the more interesting position: a living system without a mind. It is Plato’s cosmos with the Demiurge removed and the bacteria in charge.`,

  "Val Plumwood, Feminism and the Mastery of Nature": `Being Prey is the essay in this atlas I would put beside Pascal’s thinking reed. Pascal says that even if the universe crushes him he is nobler than it because he knows. Plumwood, in the crocodile’s jaws, discovers that she is meat. The second is the more truthful account of where we stand.`,

  "Philippe Descola, Beyond Nature and Culture": `Descola gave me the most useful map for this atlas: interiority and physicality. Descartes is the textbook naturalist, and the Renaissance magi, with their correspondences, are analogists. The Achuar treat animals as persons. The governing question looks different once one sees that our answer to it is a choice made in seventeenth-century Europe.`,

  "Viveiros de Castro, Amerindian perspectivism": `Perspectivism is the most radical inversion in the atlas. For Descartes, we share bodies with animals and differ in mind; for the Amazonian peoples, we share mind with animals and differ in body. I cannot inhabit their world, but knowing it exists makes me less sure that ours was discovered rather than chosen.`,

  "Bruno Latour, We Have Never Been Modern": `Latour is the one who saw that Boyle and Hobbes, in the same decade, made the founding division: nature to the laboratory, society to the sovereign. If we have never been modern, then the whole atlas is a history of a story the moderns told about themselves while doing something else. His Gaia lectures are the place where the occult’s living cosmos and climate science meet.`,

  "Jane Bennett and Karen Barad, the new materialism": `The new materialists are Cavendish and Campanella in the language of contemporary theory: matter is lively, perceptive in its way, never dead. I am sympathetic and uneasy. Bennett’s blackout is persuasive, but a world in which the grid acted is also a world in which the utility company is off the hook.`,

  "Harman and Morton, object-oriented ontology": `I gave this entry the picture of nature as veil, which may surprise. But Harman’s objects that withdraw from every relation are a strange return of the hidden nature of Heraclitus and of the Upaniṣadic inner controller that earth does not know. Morton’s idea of the hyperobject, finally, names why climate change resists every older picture in this atlas: it is too large to appear.`,

  "Thomas Nagel, Mind and Cosmos": `Nagel names the source of the problem exactly: the seventeenth century built its picture of nature by leaving the mind out, so the mind cannot be put back in without changing the picture. That is the thread of this whole atlas in one sentence. His willingness to consider natural teleology, three hundred years after Bacon banished it, is the most surprising development in recent analytic philosophy.`,

  "Panpsychism revived: Strawson and Goff": `Goff’s title, <i>Galileo’s Error</i>, closes a circle that opened with the tickling feather in this atlas. Panpsychism is Cavendish, Leibniz and Campanella again, defended with the tools of analytic philosophy. I do not know if it is true. I do know that the alternatives all accept Galileo’s exclusion and then struggle to undo it.`,

  "Dipesh Chakrabarty, The Climate of History": `Chakrabarty is the right voice to bring the atlas from Bengal and Bacon into the present. He shows that the governing question has changed its grammar: we ask not only what we may do to nature, but what we have become by doing it. A geological agent is not a steward or a master; it is a force like a volcano that has a conscience.`,

  "Yuk Hui, cosmotechnics": `Hui answers the question I asked of the Chinese section: why did Xunzi’s mastery not become Bacon’s? Because technique was held within the relation of vessel and Way. His proposal, that every technology carries a cosmology, makes this whole atlas into a resource for engineering, which is how I would like my students to read it.`,

  "Robin Wall Kimmerer, Braiding Sweetgrass": `Kimmerer’s grammar of animacy is the simplest reversal of Descartes I know: change the pronoun and you change the world. Her rules for the Honourable Harvest are almost exactly Ḥayy ibn Yaqẓān’s rules for eating: take the least, preserve the seed, do not take the last. Two traditions separated by eight centuries and an ocean found the same ethic.`,

  "Pope Francis, Laudato si’": `Laudato si’ is the Church answering Lynn White fifty years later, and its paragraph 67 is the reading of Genesis I gave at the start of that section. The phrase technocratic paradigm is Heidegger’s enframing rendered for bishops. It is striking that the most widely read critique of Baconianism in our century came from Rome.`,

  "Bostrom and transhumanism": `Transhumanism is Bacon’s effecting of all things possible applied to the human body itself, and Pico’s Adam without a fixed nature turned into a research programme. C. S. Lewis predicted exactly this in 1943: the last conquest is of human nature, by those who would condition it. I find the transhumanists honest and their premise untenable, since nothing in their account tells us what to want once we can want anything.`,

  "Machine minds, from Turing to the present": `This is the last entry because it brings the atlas back to its beginning. Descartes denied minds to animals because they could not answer whatever is said to them; we now have machines that can. The mechanical picture of nature has produced, at its end, machines that put the Cartesian question to us. I write this as someone who works alongside such machines every day, and I do not know the answer.`,
};
